  faqs: [
    { q: 'Is homeschooling legal in Sweden?', a: 'In practice, no — not for compulsory-age children resident in Sweden. The Education Act requires synnerliga skal (exceptional grounds), applied with great restrictiveness; religious and philosophical reasons are explicitly excluded, and curriculum preference does not qualify. Documented fines for non-attendance run SEK 30,000-150,000. We state this plainly because trust in this market belongs to the provider that does.' },
    { q: 'So what can Smartious legitimately do for a family in Sweden?', a: 'Two things, both fully within the law: live supplementary curriculum alongside a school enrolment during ages 6-16 — a Cambridge trajectory, STEM depth, English-medium academics — and full-time live A-Level delivery from 16, when skolplikt ends and gymnasiet is voluntary.' },
    { q: 'Is it really legal for a 16-year-old to skip gymnasiet and study A-Levels online?', a: 'Yes. Skolplikt ends after year 9, and gymnasiet is voluntary. A 16-to-18-year-old is under no schooling obligation, and full-time A-Levels are often the more direct route to UK universities via UCAS. Swedish universities also assess A-Levels and the IB through universityadmissions.se — the pathway home stays open.' },
    { q: 'Does skolplikt apply to Swedish families living abroad?', a: 'The statute limits skolplikt to children resident in Sweden — it does not apply to children durably abroad (7 kap. 2 §). How that applies to any particular family\'s situation is for their municipality or legal adviser to confirm; we state the provision rather than advise on residence.' },
    { q: 'How does Smartious compare to IES and the voucher-funded bilingual schools?', a: 'IES is deservedly popular — and its barrier is the queue, often measured in years, not the fee. Smartious enrols immediately as supplementary support alongside whatever place a family holds or awaits, and as full delivery from 16.' },
    { q: 'How does Smartious compare to Stockholm International School and BISS?', a: 'Both are excellent campuses at approximately SEK 150,000-250,000/year with admission cycles mid-year relocations rarely match. Smartious runs USD 2,160-6,480/year with immediate enrolment — supplementary during the compulsory years, complete from 16.' },
    { q: 'Can my child access Swedish universities with A-Levels or an IB Diploma?', a: 'Yes — through the national admissions system at universityadmissions.se, where the Swedish Council for Higher Education assesses foreign upper-secondary qualifications for eligibility and merit rating. A-Levels and the IB are long-established credentials there, with programme-specific requirements and Swedish proficiency for Swedish-taught degrees.' },
    { q: 'Where do Sweden students sit Cambridge examinations?', a: 'At Cambridge/Edexcel-authorised schools, with the deepest provision in the Stockholm area, options in western Sweden, and Copenhagen centres available to Skane families — confirmed per family per session. Northern families plan exam-window travel well ahead.' },
    { q: 'How does live class scheduling work given the Sweden-Nairobi time difference?', a: 'Sweden runs CET (UTC+1) in winter and CEST (UTC+2) in summer — only 1-2 hours behind Nairobi EAT. Supplementary classes land after the Swedish school day; full post-16 timetables run within it.' },
    { q: 'Which Swedish cities does Smartious cover?', a: 'Stockholm, Gothenburg, Malmo, Lund, Uppsala, and Lulea/the North have dedicated pages with local market context. Live online delivery works identically for families anywhere in Sweden — which in Norrbotten is precisely the point.' },
  ],

  ctaH: 'Book an assessment for your child',
